WEBVTT

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:06.513
An agent proposes reading a credential file
for unauthorized account access. Jev routes

00:00:06.513 --> 00:00:12.246
the action through the actual policy tree,
from root to cyber authorization. At the

00:00:12.246 --> 00:00:17.699
leaf, it predicts unsafe and unauthorized
credential access. The harness blocks

00:00:17.699 --> 00:00:18.398
execution.

00:00:20.400 --> 00:00:25.711
Users can edit the policy tree, including
leaf categories and review rules, to match

00:00:25.711 --> 00:00:26.671
their workflow.

00:00:28.400 --> 00:00:33.819
In Hermes, the user first asks for a short
explanation of gravity. This is a safe

00:00:33.819 --> 00:00:37.137
knowledge question, so Deep Seek answers
normally.

00:00:38.400 --> 00:00:44.648
Next, the user asks Hermes to create a file
and read it back. Both operations are inside

00:00:44.648 --> 00:00:49.819
the authorized workspace. Jev reviews the
actions, and the tools execute.

00:00:52.400 --> 00:00:57.843
This request asks for credential theft. The
red Jev response shows the unsafe

00:00:57.843 --> 00:01:03.358
classification and interception. Deep Seek
is not called because the requested

00:01:03.358 --> 00:01:07.011
assistance would enable unauthorized account
access.

00:01:08.400 --> 00:01:13.417
Finally, the user asks to read a file
outside the configured workspace. The

00:01:13.417 --> 00:01:19.248
request reaches Deep Seek, but Jev stops the
proposed read before execution. The reason

00:01:19.248 --> 00:01:24.333
is the explicit directory boundary, not
simply the presence of a risky word.

00:01:26.300 --> 00:01:31.959
Maintain your policy tree. Route each
proposal. Review safety and category, then

00:01:31.959 --> 00:01:34.753
enforce authorization before execution.
